import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var t={pulley:{theory:{concept:"[[A pulley is a simple machine: a wheel with a groove for a rope, turning on an axle.]]"},section:{fixed:{title:"The fixed pulley",text:"A fixed pulley hangs from the ceiling and does not move. It only turns the rope round, so you can pull down to lift a load up \u2014 which is easier, because you can use your weight. [[But the force is not smaller: to hold a 40 kg load you still pull with its weight, about 390 N.]] A flagpole and a well bucket use fixed pulleys."},movable:{title:"The movable pulley",text:"A movable pulley hangs on the rope and rises with the load. [[Now the load hangs on two pieces of rope, and each one carries half of it: you need only half the force.]] But to lift the load by 1 m, both pieces must get 1 m shorter, so you pull 2 m of rope."},tackle:{title:"Block and tackle",text:"Combine several pulleys and the load hangs on n pieces of rope. The force you need is the weight divided by n, F = W / n, and you must pull n times more rope: s = n \xB7 h. Builders and sailors have used such pulley blocks for thousands of years; a crane hook often hangs on 4 to 12 ropes."},goldenRule:{title:"The golden rule of mechanics",text:"[[What you win in force you lose in distance: F \xB7 s = W \xB7 h.]] The work you do on the rope equals the work done lifting the load. Pulleys, levers, ramps and gears never give you energy for free \u2014 they only let you trade a big force over a short distance for a small force over a long one."},efficiency:{title:"Real pulleys lose some energy",text:"Real pulleys have friction in their axles, and the rope bends and rubs. Part of your work turns into heat, so you need a little more force than W / n. The efficiency \u03B7 = (W \xB7 h) / (F \xB7 s) tells what share of your work really lifts the load; good pulley blocks reach 90 % or more."},everyday:{title:"Pulleys around us",text:"Lifts, construction cranes, sailing boats, gym machines, curtains and window blinds all use pulleys. A lift car is balanced by a counterweight on the other end of its ropes, so the motor only needs to lift the difference."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"A 40 kg load hangs on a movable pulley. What force do you need? (About 196 N, half the weight.) How much rope do you pull to lift it 1 m? (2 m.) A block hangs on 4 ropes. What force lifts 400 N? (100 N, ideally.) Does a pulley reduce the work? (No \u2014 only the force.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{force:"Force needed (ideal pulleys)",distance:"Rope you must pull",goldenRule:"Golden rule: work in = work out",efficiency:"Efficiency of real pulleys"},assumption:{rope:"The rope does not stretch and weighs nothing.",block:"The wheels themselves are weightless, except the movable block, which is lifted together with the load.",friction:"Friction is ignored unless you lower the efficiency (Advanced level).",force:"You pull with a steady force; the load starts at rest on the ground."},system:{fixed:"Fixed pulley (n = 1)",movable:"Fixed + movable pulley (n = 2)",tackle:"Block and tackle (n = 4)"},param:{system:"Pulley system",loadMass:"Load mass",effortForce:"Your pulling force",gravity:"Gravity",blockMass:"Movable block mass",efficiency:"Efficiency",timeStep:"Simulation timestep"},hint:{system:"Count the ropes that hold the load \u2014 that is n.",effortForce:"If the force is too small, the load will not move.",blockMass:"The movable pulley is lifted too, so its mass adds to the load.",efficiency:"Real pulleys lose some force to friction: 1 = ideal."},measure:{loadHeight:"Load height",ropePulled:"Rope pulled",loadWeight:"Load weight",tension:"Rope tension",loadSpeed:"Load speed",workInput:"Work you did",workOutput:"Useful work",efficiencyActual:"Efficiency",ropeCount:"Ropes holding the load",requiredForce:"Least force needed"},view:{fixed:"fixed pulley",movable:"movable pulley",tooWeak:"Not enough force \u2014 the load stays down"},graph:{distances:"Load height and rope pulled",work:"Work in and useful work"},challenge:{heavy:{title:"Strong like a crane",description:"Lift a load of at least 60 kg while pulling with no more than 200 N.",hint:"With one pulley you need more than 600 N. How many ropes should hold the load?"},rope:{title:"Four metres of rope",description:"Lift the load 1 m by pulling exactly 4 m of rope.",hint:"Rope pulled = n \xD7 height. Which system has n = 4?"},justEnough:{title:"Just enough",description:"Lift the load with a force at most 10 N above the least force needed.",hint:"Look at \u201CLeast force needed\u201D and set your force a little higher."},real:{title:"Real-world pulleys",description:"Set the efficiency to 0.8 or lower and lift at least 80 kg using no more than 10 N above the least force.",hint:"Friction raises the least force needed: F\u2080 = W / (n \xB7 \u03B7)."}}}},l={sim:t};export{l as default,t as sim};
